WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.980 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.455 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.455 --> 00:00:07.425 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.425 --> 00:00:18.050 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.690 align:middle line:84%
VASILY STRELA: The rest of the
class, let's talk a little bit

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:26.780 align:middle line:84%
about interest rates and
a little bit about bonds,

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:33.650 align:middle line:84%
because that's very important
and central for many--

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.860 align:middle line:84%
will be definitely
part of many classes

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:43.610 align:middle line:84%
and is generally central
for finance, and for life,

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.520 align:middle line:90%
in my opinion.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.350 align:middle line:90%
So what is interest?

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.910 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's start with now.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:55.310 align:middle line:90%


00:00:55.310 --> 00:01:00.170 align:middle line:84%
And suppose we have
$1 which we invested--

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.010 align:middle line:90%
well, put it in the bank.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.810 align:middle line:90%
So what happens in one year?

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:08.180 align:middle line:90%


00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.673 align:middle line:84%
Well, you expect to get your
dollar back with some interest.

00:01:12.673 --> 00:01:13.590 align:middle line:90%
And what does it mean?

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.980 align:middle line:90%
So you get 1 plus something.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.110 align:middle line:90%
I don't know--

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.620 align:middle line:90%
5% nowadays.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.110 align:middle line:84%
And this r is called
interest rate.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.490 align:middle line:84%
So what happens if
you reinvest it.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.950 align:middle line:90%
What will you get?

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.840 align:middle line:90%
The whole thing.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:36.690 align:middle line:84%
So in two years you would expect
to get 1 plus r times 1 plus r.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.070 align:middle line:90%
So 1 plus r squared.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:55.820 align:middle line:84%
So in n years, you will expect
to get 1 plus r to the power n.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:01.170 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's make it small and
small not to confuse ourselves.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:06.120 align:middle line:84%
So now let's make life a
little bit more complicated.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:13.640 align:middle line:84%
What happens if you decide to
take your money in half a year?

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.420 align:middle line:90%
You had one here.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.790 align:middle line:90%
So what happens in half a year?

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.020 align:middle line:84%
Well, you get half
of the interest.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.880 align:middle line:90%
So it will be 1 plus r over 2.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:31.440 align:middle line:84%
And in one year, it will
be 1 plus r over 2 squared

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.080 align:middle line:90%
because you reinvest it.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.440 align:middle line:84%
So suppose you keep
it in the bank.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:44.150 align:middle line:84%
And in n years, it will be
1 plus r over 2 but twice n.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:47.910 align:middle line:84%
Because there are
twice as many periods

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.280 align:middle line:90%
Let's subdivide further.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.340 align:middle line:90%
Let's make m period.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:58.700 align:middle line:84%
So in the first period,
you get 1 over--

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.630 align:middle line:90%
1 plus r over m.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:10.730 align:middle line:84%
In one year, it will be 1
plus r over m to the power m.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:19.160 align:middle line:84%
And here, you'll get
1 plus r over m--

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.410 align:middle line:90%
m times m.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.210 align:middle line:90%


00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:26.690 align:middle line:90%
What if we take the limit.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:30.980 align:middle line:84%
If we are able to reinvest
every second, every millisecond,

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.750 align:middle line:90%
what happens then?

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:40.316 align:middle line:84%
So we'll send this
m to the infinity.

00:03:40.316 --> 00:03:48.710 align:middle line:90%


00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:54.050 align:middle line:84%
So what is-- who
knows what is that?

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:54.980 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: e.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:58.600 align:middle line:84%
VASILY STRELA:
Well, it is e, but--

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.440 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: r.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.310 align:middle line:90%
VASILY STRELA: r.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:02.425 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, sorry. r.

00:04:02.425 --> 00:04:03.300 align:middle line:90%
VASILY STRELA: Right.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:08.402 align:middle line:84%
r has to be there, and
this n will stay there.

00:04:08.402 --> 00:04:09.570 align:middle line:90%
Well, yeah.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.140 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, if we keep m here.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.670 align:middle line:84%
So in fact, interesting
historical fact, that's how e

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.200 align:middle line:90%
was discovered and defined.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:28.400 align:middle line:84%
It was Bernoulli in his
seminal paper in 1683,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.370 align:middle line:84%
where he described
this computation.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:36.890 align:middle line:84%
And it was about
interest rates, about how

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:38.640 align:middle line:90%
interest is compounding.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.380 align:middle line:84%
So maybe this was the
first mathematical finance

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.640 align:middle line:90%
paper in the history.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:52.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's change the game a
little bit-- not the game--

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.540 align:middle line:84%
change the question
a little bit.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:05:03.775 align:middle line:84%
So suppose we know that we
will receive $1 in one year.

00:05:03.775 --> 00:05:08.870 align:middle line:90%


00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:15.450 align:middle line:84%
How much should we pay for this
right to receive $1 in one year?

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:20.580 align:middle line:84%
How much should we pay now
to receive $1 in one year?

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.480 align:middle line:90%
And let's compute it.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.820 align:middle line:90%
So suppose we don't know.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:29.930 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that
now it is x dollars.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:33.540 align:middle line:84%
And let's consider the
following strategy.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:46.610 align:middle line:84%
Let's borrow $1-- let's
borrow x, this amount now,

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:50.400 align:middle line:84%
and let's see what
happens at the end.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.640 align:middle line:84%
So at the end, in one year, I
mean, we'll receive $1 for sure.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:01.960 align:middle line:84%
And we'll have to repay
our debt but with interest.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:08.985 align:middle line:84%
So we'll have repay
1 plus x times r.

00:06:08.985 --> 00:06:12.670 align:middle line:90%


00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:15.865 align:middle line:90%
Sorry-- x times 1 plus.

00:06:15.865 --> 00:06:19.450 align:middle line:90%


00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:24.430 align:middle line:90%
Now, what should this be?

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:28.660 align:middle line:84%
Can it be greater than
zero, this difference?

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:33.170 align:middle line:84%
Well, if it's greater than
zero, why wouldn't we borrow--

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:37.870 align:middle line:84%
why wouldn't we buy a right
to receive a billion dollars,

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:42.430 align:middle line:90%
then we'll have a lot of profit?

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.090 align:middle line:90%
Can it be less than zero?

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:46.600 align:middle line:84%
Well, then we would
be stupid to enter

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.660 align:middle line:84%
this contract because
we'll lose money for sure.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:52.130 align:middle line:84%
We know that this will
happen in one year.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:55.690 align:middle line:90%
So for no arbitrage--

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.850 align:middle line:84%
and we don't assume that
our counterparty is stupid.

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.400 align:middle line:84%
We don't think
that we are stupid.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.150 align:middle line:90%
So it has to be zero.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:09.610 align:middle line:90%
And this x is equal to 1 plus--

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.970 align:middle line:90%
divided by 1 plus r.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:17.170 align:middle line:84%
And this is called
discounting, meaning

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:21.940 align:middle line:84%
that if we know that we'll
receive a certain amount

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:25.840 align:middle line:84%
in the future, and we know
the interest rates, prevailing

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:32.950 align:middle line:84%
interest rates, then we have
to discount the future value

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:36.860 align:middle line:84%
to today using a
discount factor.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:41.450 align:middle line:84%
And this is the discount factor
assuming annualized compounding.

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:47.560 align:middle line:84%
If we assume
annualized compounding

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:52.180 align:middle line:84%
and receive n dollars
in the future,

00:07:52.180 --> 00:08:00.100 align:middle line:84%
in the future n years, the value
of n dollars in the future,

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:03.610 align:middle line:84%
it is the value of
our n dollars, which

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:05.900 align:middle line:84%
we are receiving in
the future n years,

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.530 align:middle line:84%
but it has to be
discounted by n years.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:09.720 align:middle line:90%
So it should be--

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:14.710 align:middle line:90%


00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:16.670 align:middle line:90%
And here, it is discounting.

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:20.620 align:middle line:84%
If we assume a continuous
discounting, something

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:23.890 align:middle line:90%
like this, then it will be--

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:33.549 align:middle line:84%
our price will be n
times e to the minus rn.

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:36.549 align:middle line:84%
This will be our
discount factor,

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:39.640 align:middle line:90%
assuming continuous compounding.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.090 align:middle line:84%
And we already
hitting the problem--

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:44.030 align:middle line:90%
or not the problem.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.570 align:middle line:84%
But we are already
hitting a situation

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:57.400 align:middle line:84%
where some model is
needed, which brings us

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:02.140 align:middle line:84%
to the world of financial
instruments-- interest rate

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:06.040 align:middle line:90%
related financial instruments.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:09.370 align:middle line:84%
And the simplest
financial instrument

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:11.330 align:middle line:90%
is a zero-coupon bond.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.500 align:middle line:84%
That's exactly the
right to receive

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.650 align:middle line:90%
certain amount of notional.

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:22.330 align:middle line:84%
Or notional-- or quite often
1 or 100 in certain amount

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:24.072 align:middle line:90%
of time.

00:09:24.072 --> 00:09:28.900 align:middle line:84%
And that's the value of
our zero-coupon bond.

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:33.920 align:middle line:84%
More common financial
instruments are coupon bonds.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.470 align:middle line:84%
Because most people would like
to receive periodic interest

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:41.720 align:middle line:90%
for lending money.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:44.180 align:middle line:84%
And that's effectively
lending money.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.000 align:middle line:90%
And this is typical bonds.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.210 align:middle line:84%
For example, if you
buy government bonds

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.560 align:middle line:90%
that's what will happen.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:57.010 align:middle line:84%
You'll give the government
your $100, and government

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:59.960 align:middle line:84%
will be repaying you
coupons periodically,

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:03.620 align:middle line:84%
usually semiannually,
here in the US--

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.810 align:middle line:84%
in Europe, it's quite often
quarterly-- and the notional,

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:10.100 align:middle line:90%
all your $100, at the end.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:17.680 align:middle line:84%
So what the price of such
a coupon bond should be?

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:25.120 align:middle line:84%
Well, we'll need to take all the
cash flows and discount them.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.320 align:middle line:84%
And if we assume a
constant interest rate,

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:35.170 align:middle line:84%
we'll have to discount it with
our constant discount factors.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:37.760 align:middle line:90%
And that's how much--

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:44.030 align:middle line:84%
and this assumes-- well, say
annual coupons and annual rate.

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:48.250 align:middle line:84%
So that will be your
sequence with a notional

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:52.450 align:middle line:84%
at the end, the whole
big notional at the end.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.780 align:middle line:90%
And can we sum this up?

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.050 align:middle line:90%
Well, sure.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:00.560 align:middle line:90%
That's geometric sequence.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.260 align:middle line:84%
Everybody can sum up
geometric sequence.

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:06.740 align:middle line:90%
And that's the value.

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:07.800 align:middle line:90%
That's what you get.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:10.900 align:middle line:90%


00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:13.420 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
value of the bond.

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:20.110 align:middle line:84%
Now, the bonds are
traded on the markets.

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:22.630 align:middle line:90%
There are prices of the bonds.

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:28.240 align:middle line:84%
And if you start investing
into bonds, and not in just

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.380 align:middle line:84%
in one bond, you should be
able to compare to bonds.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:36.970 align:middle line:84%
And comparing the price
is not really convenient

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:40.750 align:middle line:84%
because bonds have
different maturities.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:44.740 align:middle line:84%
Even with the same interest,
would have different prices

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:47.660 align:middle line:84%
because there will be
different number of cash flows.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:51.430 align:middle line:84%
Bonds of different coupons
will have different prices.

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:56.560 align:middle line:84%
So what turns out to be
more convenient measure

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.730 align:middle line:84%
to compare bonds and various
fixed income securities

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:01.880 align:middle line:90%
is yield.

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:07.360 align:middle line:84%
And yield is such
a constant interest

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:14.020 align:middle line:84%
rate, which by this discounting
gives you market price.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:15.950 align:middle line:84%
So you buy something
at the market price.

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:17.150 align:middle line:90%
You know the market price.

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:20.090 align:middle line:90%
So all the cash flows.

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:23.450 align:middle line:84%
So you back out one
flat interest rate,

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:28.610 align:middle line:84%
which will give you
the price of the bond.

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:31.190 align:middle line:90%
That's called yield of the bond.

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:34.390 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, there is
one-to-one relationship

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:35.720 align:middle line:90%
between price and yield.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:44.030 align:middle line:84%
So for our zero-coupon bond,
yield is pretty simple.

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:48.370 align:middle line:90%
You just invert this formula.

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:50.650 align:middle line:90%
And this is here on the slide.

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:55.060 align:middle line:84%
For coupon bonds,
yield computation

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:57.790 align:middle line:84%
is more complex
because inverting

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:01.550 align:middle line:90%
this formula is more involved.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:03.530 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, it has
to be numerical.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:06.853 align:middle line:84%
But that's how
yields are computed.

00:13:06.853 --> 00:13:09.550 align:middle line:90%


00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:17.620 align:middle line:84%
Here, I'll I give you
an example of a few--

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:22.480 align:middle line:84%
of price yield relationship for
a 10-year bond with different

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:23.650 align:middle line:90%
coupons.

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:27.380 align:middle line:84%
So what's important to know here
is that the higher the coupon--

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:32.050 align:middle line:84%
and that's natural--
the higher the yield

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:35.560 align:middle line:84%
is for the same bond
of the same maturity.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:39.280 align:middle line:84%
But what is important to
know is the yield and price

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:40.940 align:middle line:90%
are in inverse relationship.

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:45.310 align:middle line:84%
The high yield, the lower
the price because it's

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:47.830 align:middle line:90%
all about discounting.

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:49.520 align:middle line:90%
It's in the denominator.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:52.450 align:middle line:84%
The higher the price,
the lower the yield.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:56.260 align:middle line:90%


00:13:56.260 --> 00:14:01.235 align:middle line:90%
So that's what it is.

00:14:01.235 --> 00:14:07.780 align:middle line:84%
But real life is more
complicated than that.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:11.080 align:middle line:84%
In real world, as if
we wanted everything

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:14.530 align:middle line:84%
to be the same
simple interest rate,

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:19.720 align:middle line:84%
and all bonds have to
be discounted the same,

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:21.470 align:middle line:90%
real life is slightly different.

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:24.280 align:middle line:90%
So if you take markets--

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:28.820 align:middle line:84%
securities, market bonds and
start breaking up their yields,

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:34.750 align:middle line:84%
they will not be constant,
particularly with maturity.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:37.100 align:middle line:84%
And this is called
the yield curve.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.800 align:middle line:90%
And I gave you--

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:45.700 align:middle line:84%
here, I plotted a yield
curve for a 10-year--

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:51.610 align:middle line:84%
term structure of 10-year
yields, US government yields,

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:53.210 align:middle line:90%
for a variety of times.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.930 align:middle line:84%
And I want to talk just
a little bit about it

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:57.260 align:middle line:90%
because it's quite topical.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:59.900 align:middle line:84%
So that's the
thickest line is this.

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:09.121 align:middle line:84%
That's the yield curve for
September 3, so two days ago.

00:15:09.121 --> 00:15:10.790 align:middle line:90%
So quite different.

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:15.760 align:middle line:84%
And yields here for a very
short-dated securities

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:18.740 align:middle line:84%
are quite higher than for
long-dated securities.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:21.470 align:middle line:90%
This is still quite atypical.

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:26.590 align:middle line:84%
For example, in '92, around
the same September time,

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:30.160 align:middle line:90%
yield curve looked like this--

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:33.800 align:middle line:84%
considered to be more
or less classical curve.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:38.260 align:middle line:84%
And generally speaking, we
expect upward-sloping curves

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:40.960 align:middle line:84%
because generally
speaking, people

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:46.280 align:middle line:84%
would like to receive more yield
for a longer term investments.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:53.080 align:middle line:84%
And that's what
was the case in--

00:15:53.080 --> 00:16:00.520 align:middle line:90%
this is what-- this is '21.

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:04.630 align:middle line:90%
This is '14.

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:07.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, nice looking curves.

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:10.480 align:middle line:84%
The only difference here
is that now they are--

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:16.450 align:middle line:84%
in '92, they were grounded at 3%
and went all the way up to 7%,

00:16:16.450 --> 00:16:24.460 align:middle line:84%
those were grounded at zero
and went only to 2%, 3%.

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:29.020 align:middle line:84%
Now, interesting
observation here is--

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:32.620 align:middle line:84%
let's look at this
inverted curves.

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:35.500 align:middle line:90%
A year ago it was inverted.

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:37.900 align:middle line:90%
Now it is inverted.

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:41.520 align:middle line:90%
It was inverted in 2007.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:47.100 align:middle line:84%
No, this is 2019,
and this is 2007.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:54.010 align:middle line:84%
So what the-- do we have
anything in your mind

00:16:54.010 --> 00:17:04.140 align:middle line:84%
what's common between
2021 and 2007?

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:05.863 align:middle line:90%
So--

00:17:05.863 --> 00:17:07.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The year
before the recession.

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:09.190 align:middle line:90%
VASILY STRELA: Yes, exactly.

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:13.859 align:middle line:84%
Well, 2007, the recession
was "the recession."

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:17.890 align:middle line:84%
2021-- there was a
recession in 2020.

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:21.310 align:middle line:84%
Even this yield curve
is before COVID.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:23.339 align:middle line:90%
So nothing was--

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:28.470 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at the shape of
the yield curves and recession

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:32.620 align:middle line:84%
times, well, it is
common analogy--

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:35.340 align:middle line:90%
and there are papers on that--

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:41.880 align:middle line:84%
how predictive is inverted
yield curve for the recessions?

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:46.300 align:middle line:84%
Generally, it is assumed
to be predictive.

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:47.800 align:middle line:90%
So here we go.

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:49.540 align:middle line:90%
We have an inverted yield curve.

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:52.980 align:middle line:84%
What does it tell
us about the future?

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:55.905 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's all
Fed's job to keep it--

00:17:55.905 --> 00:17:58.800 align:middle line:90%


00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:04.060 align:middle line:84%
to make it a soft landing and
for a recession not to happen.

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:05.050 align:middle line:90%
And we'll see.

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:07.660 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it will be, or maybe not.

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:10.290 align:middle line:84%
But what I want to
emphasize that there

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:13.020 align:middle line:84%
is a lot of information
in yield curves.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:15.450 align:middle line:84%
One of the lectures in the
future, Andrew Gunstensen,

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:17.940 align:middle line:84%
he will be talking
mostly about yield curves

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:21.240 align:middle line:84%
and yield curve constructions
and the instruments around that.

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:29.430 align:middle line:84%
So it's very important
piece of knowledge.

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:32.790 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, I'll promise you,
when I come back later on

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:36.360 align:middle line:84%
to talk about Black-Scholes,
I'll bring this slide back,

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:39.100 align:middle line:84%
and we'll see what happens
with the yield curve.

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:41.850 align:middle line:84%
Because what-- and
I can guarantee you

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:44.670 align:middle line:84%
it will look different
because the Fed

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:47.860 align:middle line:84%
is happening-- the Fed meeting
is happening in September.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:53.880 align:middle line:84%
Everybody expects the yields
to be cut, which immediately

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:56.380 align:middle line:90%
will bring the short end down.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:57.880 align:middle line:90%
What happens with the long end?

00:18:57.880 --> 00:18:58.730 align:middle line:90%
I wish I knew.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:01.320 align:middle line:90%


00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:05.040 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, that's
about yield curves.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:09.060 align:middle line:84%
But another important
thing to emphasize that no

00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:18.490 align:middle line:84%
matter what, bond prices are
sensitive to yields, obviously.

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:22.660 align:middle line:84%
And they are sensitive to
yields in nonlinear fashion.

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:23.800 align:middle line:90%
What does it mean?

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:29.130 align:middle line:84%
Well, it means that if you hold
bonds on your hands and yields

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:34.480 align:middle line:84%
change, and they do change,
your investment will change.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.570 align:middle line:84%
So it's quite
important to understand

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:38.790 align:middle line:84%
the sensitivity
of your investment

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:41.630 align:middle line:90%
to interest rates and yields.

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:44.235 align:middle line:90%


00:19:44.235 --> 00:19:45.730 align:middle line:90%
And what are sensitivities?

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:48.256 align:middle line:90%
Those are derivatives.

00:19:48.256 --> 00:19:51.570 align:middle line:84%
Or in finance and
fixed income world,

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:56.130 align:middle line:84%
they're called durations because
they're usually scaled by price.

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:59.430 align:middle line:84%
So very important
measures are duration

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:06.270 align:middle line:84%
of the bond, which is derivative
with respect to yield.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:11.277 align:middle line:84%
And for each different bond
and each different security,

00:20:11.277 --> 00:20:12.610 align:middle line:90%
they will be slightly different.

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:17.260 align:middle line:84%
And not only the first
derivative is important,

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:22.350 align:middle line:84%
the second derivative or
convexity is also important.

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:24.660 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:31.770 align:middle line:84%
And that's what we'll be talking
about in various classes.

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:37.950 align:middle line:84%
We will be talking about more
interesting interest rate

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:40.650 align:middle line:84%
objects in the future, in
particular, forward rates

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:41.560 align:middle line:90%
and swaps.

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:49.770 align:middle line:84%
But I'll leave it to Stefan and
Andrew to talk more about that.

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:54.130 align:middle line:90%
That's probably all for today.

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:57.365 align:middle line:90%
And any questions to any of us?

00:20:57.365 --> 00:21:03.780 align:middle line:90%


00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:07.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Have you done this--
have you done that game before?

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:09.820 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER: Not specifically
in this format.

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:13.630 align:middle line:84%
So we tried-- before
it was more open-ended.

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:17.580 align:middle line:84%
I was thinking-- so I
think by fixing the dates,

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:21.300 align:middle line:84%
you don't have to make the
market timing decision.

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:22.950 align:middle line:90%
We already made it for you.

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:24.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:36.000 align:middle line:90%